## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform design, policy-as-code, drift handling, and test automation at scale—core for Oracle, Tesla, and LinkedIn-like environments.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliasing for multi-region/multi-cloud setups
- Wrapper modules to standardize tagging and policy constraints
- Terraform Cloud/Sentinel or OPA for governance
- Drift detection workflows and isolated drift environments
- End-to-end testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example

```hcl
# Wrapper module injecting standard tags
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
variable "tags" { type = map(string) }

module "service" {
  source = "../aws-azure-base"
  providers = {
    aws   = aws.us_east_1
    aws_eu = aws.eu_west_1
    azurerm = azurerm.westus2
  }
  tags = merge(var.tags, {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you structure a Sentinel policy to enforce tag requirements and deny secrets in state?
- How would you simulate drift in tests and verify policy compliance across clouds?

This question validates the ability to compose modules with environment-driven data while avoiding duplication and maintaining clean state separation. It covers essential skills including Terraform's external data source and locals for environment-specific configuration, for_each to create per-environment resources from a map, wrapper modules for consistent tagging (Environment, CostCenter), per-environment backend configuration for isolated state, and CI gates with fmt/validate/tflint.

## Code Example
```terraform
# ip_allocations.json
{
  "dev": "10.0.0.0/16",
  "stage": "10.1.0.0/16",
  "prod": "10.2.0.0/16"
}
```

```terraform
# main.tf (external data source + locals + subnet module usage)
data "external" "ip_allocations" {
  program = ["python3", "${path.module}/read_ip_allocations.py"]
}

locals {
  env_cidr = jsondecode(data.external.ip_allocations.result)
}

module "subnets" {
  source   = "./modules/subnets"
  for_each = toset(["dev", "stage", "prod"])

  vpc_id     = module.vpc.vpc_id
  cidr_block = local.env_cidr[each.key]
  environment = each.key
}
```

```terraform
# wrapper module (tags)
# modules/vpc-wrapper/main.tf
locals {
  default_tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  }
}

module "vpc" {
  source = "../shared-vpc"

  tags = merge(local.default_tags, var.extra_tags)
}
```

```terraform
# backend.tf (per-environment)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    key = "${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design a scalable drift-resilient Terraform workflow across multiple AWS accounts while ensuring safe automation and auditable infrastructure changes.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection, automatic remediation, policy enforcement (Sentinel/OPA)
- Environment-specific remote backends, provider aliasing, central baseline state
- Module architecture boundaries, testing frameworks (Kitchen-Terraform/Terratest), CI/CD gates

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "dev"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a GitOps, multi-cloud per-PR sandbox architecture with isolated backends, secret management, and policy gates. It also probes automation for ephemeral environments and teardown reliability.

## Key Concepts
- Per-PR sandbox architecture across AWS, Azure, GCP
- Provider aliases per cloud and per PR
- Per-PR remote backends and state isolation
- Vault-backed credentials with rotation
- Encryption at rest and in transit
- Policy-as-code gates (OPA/Sentinel) in CI
- Teardown automation on PR lifecycle

## Code Example
```hcl
# Minimal scaffolding
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws" }
    azurerm = { source = "hashicorp/azurerm" }
    google = { source = "hashicorp/google" }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "pr1"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "azurerm" {
  alias    = "pr1"
  features = {}
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "pr1"
  project = "pr-12345"
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-PR teardown if resources are tainted or fail during apply?
- What strategies ensure Vault rotation never disrupts in-flight deployments while keeping state secure?
